{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Conditional Statements in PHP"}, {"type": "position", "content": 10}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "You can use these words to talk about the conditional statements."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Understand information about web applications, their principles and basic concepts in a range of  work contexts.   Describe applications for an operating system for personal computers in a range of  work contexts. "}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.\t Exchanges simple information on web applications, and applications for operating systems for personal computers."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n6le02ob10re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Wish + Past Form of Verb for Wishes for Present and Future"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use <strong>wish + past form of verbs</strong> to express that we would like  things to be different"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<strong>Wish + past form of verbs</strong> helps us to express that we don't like the way things are. </br></br>  It follows this structure: </br><center> Subject + <b>wish </b>+ subject + <b>past form of verb</b> + complement</center></br></br>  Examples</br> I <b>wish</b> I <b> undestood</b> the  conditional statements. </br> I <b>wish</b> my job <b>were</b> easier, but I work with conditionals in PHP. </br>  </br>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I wish the conditional statements were easier to comprehend. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "I wish you said that before! I would have gone straight to the point. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Oh. I wish I weren't so slow\u2026"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Breaking Down the <b>If...Elseif....Else</b> Statement"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "The<b> if...elseif....else statement </b>can be a little confusing, so here are some examples for you."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Remember that the <b>if...elseif....else </b>statement is used when there are more than 3 conditions. </br></br>  <b>Examples 1</b>: </br> Imagine you are playing a video game, you are asked to pick between 3 colors; red, blue and yellow. </br> The condition is that the AI of the game would never choose the same as you, it would choose the next option. </br> So, if you pick red, the AI would choose blue. If you pick blue, the AI picks yellow, and if you pick yellow, the AI picks red. </br></br>  <b>Example 2:</b> </br> You are making a letter in Microsoft word, you are using the mouse, have you noticed that the mouse cursor changes? </br> If it is over text, it looks like a capital I.</br> If it is over an icon, it turns into an arrow.</br> If you put it between the pages borderline it becomes two arrows in two boxes."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "sum up "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to conclude an idea"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: To sum up, these statements vary depending on the number of conditions."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le02ob10aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "be out of your mind "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to be crazy"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(idiom)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Are you out of your mind? Of course, they are!"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le02ob10aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "execute "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to run"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The first type, the if statement, is an action that the code executes if a condition is true."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le02ob10aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "go straight to the point "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to answer a question or explaining something directly"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(idiom)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I wish you said that before! I would have gone straight to the point."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le02ob10aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "puzzle "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to make feel confused"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I know that, but the different types of statements are what puzzle me."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le02ob10aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "picture "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to imagine"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Picture the same example as before, but now, there is a third option, it is after 6pm, the character will say \u201cGood evening\u201d"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le02ob10aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for more information about the four types of conditional statements."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Make a chart in which you include the main information of each type.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share your chart with the rest of your class. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for information about conditional statements. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I make a chart with main information about conditional statements. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share my chart with the rest of the class."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe7n6le02ob10re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<strong>Gabriel:</strong> I wish the conditional statements were easier to comprehend.</br>\n<strong>Sebastian:</strong> They are not difficult at all!</br>\n<strong>Gabriel:</strong> Are you out of your mind? Of course, they are!</br>\n<strong>Sebastian:</strong> Let me help you, and you'll see that's not true.</br>\n<strong>Gabriel:</strong> Go ahead!</br>\n<strong>Sebastian:</strong> Remember that the conditional statements are used to perform specific actions depending on certain conditions.</br>\n<strong>Gabriel:</strong> I know that, but the different types of statements are what puzzle me."}, {"time": "26000", "image": "enpe7n6le02ob10re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<strong>Sebastian:</strong> I wish you said that before! I would have gone straight to the point. The first type, the if statement, is an action that the code executes if a condition is true. Let's use video games as an example; the condition is the hour, it is before 6 pm, the character say \u201cHi\u201d, period.</br><strong>Gabriel:</strong> Okay! I got that example, but what about the if\u2026else statement? </br><strong>Sebastian:</strong> That one executes a code when a condition is true, and the other one is false. Well, let's use the same example but with a different character. If you talk to it, it will say \u201cGood morning\u201d if it is before noon, and if it is afternoon, it will say \u201cGood afternoon\u201d.</br>"}, {"time": "72000", "image": "enpe7n6le02ob10re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<strong>Gabriel:</strong> Oh, I get it. Only one of the conditions can be true at the same time, right?</br>\n<strong>Sebastian:</strong> Exactly!</br>\n<strong>Gabriel:</strong> Now, create an example using the if\u2026elseif\u2026else statement, the bare name is confusing!</br>\n<strong>Sebastian:</strong> That one is used when there are more than two conditions. Picture the same example as before, but now, there is a third option. If it is after 6pm, the character will say \u201cGood evening\u201d.</br>\n<strong>Gabriel:</strong> So, to sum up, these statements vary depending on the number of conditions.</br>\n<strong>Sebastian:</strong> Pretty much.</br>\n<strong>Gabriel:</strong> Well, I wish I weren't so slow.</br>\n<strong>Sebastian:</strong> You're not. It's easier than you think."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n6le02ob10re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n6le02ob10re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 2, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about the use of conditional statements in PHP.   "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can list the different types of conditional statements in PHP.   "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can use different types of conditional statements in PHP. "}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Do you consider important that a program performs specific actions when you write a code? Why? "}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Is it important that an expression evaluates with true or false the codes' statements? Why?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback